Example:

    int x = 0xFF;


Common use:

    bit manipulation
    memory addresses
    embedded systems
    system programming
    permissions/flags


------------------------------------------------------------
37. OCTAL
------------------------------------------------------------

Octal uses digits:

    0-7


Example:

    int x = 075;


Important:

    075

is NOT decimal 75.

It is octal 75.


------------------------------------------------------------
38. INTEGER SUFFIXES
------------------------------------------------------------

Integer constants can use suffixes.

Examples:

    100U
    100L
    100LL
    100UL
    100ULL


Meaning:

    U   → unsigned
    L   → long
    LL  → long long


Example:

    unsigned int x = 100U;

    long y = 100000L;

    long long z = 10000000000LL;


------------------------------------------------------------
39. FLOATING CONSTANT SUFFIXES
------------------------------------------------------------

Examples:

    3.14      → double
    3.14f     → float
    3.14L     → long double


Example:

    float a = 3.14f;
    double b = 3.14;
    long double c = 3.14L;


------------------------------------------------------------
40. CONSTANT vs VARIABLE
------------------------------------------------------------

Variable:

    int age = 20;

Value can be changed:

    age = 21;


Constant using const:

    const int MAX_AGE = 100;

You cannot modify MAX_AGE through that identifier.


------------------------------------------------------------
41. const QUALIFIER
------------------------------------------------------------

Syntax:

    const data_type variable = value;


Example:

    const int MAX_USERS = 100;

After initialization:

    MAX_USERS = 200;

This is not allowed.


------------------------------------------------------------
42. const POINTER BASICS
------------------------------------------------------------

You will study pointers deeply later, but understand this
distinction:

    const int *p;

means pointer to const int.

    int *const p = &x;

means const pointer to int.

    const int *const p = &x;

means const pointer to const int.


This becomes very important in C interviews.


------------------------------------------------------------
43. #define CONSTANT
------------------------------------------------------------

Another traditional way:

    #define MAX_SIZE 100


Example:

    #define PI 3.14159


Unlike a const variable, this is a preprocessor macro.

The preprocessor replaces the macro name before compilation.

Modern C code often prefers:

    const

or enumeration/constants appropriate to the situation,
because typed constants are easier to reason about.


------------------------------------------------------------
44. ENUMERATION CONSTANTS
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    enum Day
    {
        MONDAY,
        TUESDAY,
        WEDNESDAY
    };


By default:

    MONDAY    = 0
    TUESDAY   = 1
    WEDNESDAY = 2


You can specify values:

    enum Status
    {
        SUCCESS = 0,
        ERROR = 1
    };


enum is useful for representing a fixed set of named values.


------------------------------------------------------------
45. IDENTIFIER vs VARIABLE vs CONSTANT
------------------------------------------------------------

Identifier:

    Any valid name used to identify program entities.

Examples:

    age
    calculate_sum
    Student


Variable:

    An object whose stored value can change.

Example:

    int age = 20;


Constant:

    A value that should not be modified through a particular
    mechanism.

Example:

    const int MAX = 100;


------------------------------------------------------------
46. STORAGE AND MEMORY
------------------------------------------------------------

Suppose:

    int age = 22;

The variable requires storage.

Conceptually:

    Variable name
          |
          ↓
    +-----------+
    |     22    |
    +-----------+
    Memory


The compiler determines the required representation and
alignment according to the type and implementation.


------------------------------------------------------------
47. OBJECT IN C
------------------------------------------------------------

In C terminology, an object is a region of storage that can
represent a value.

Example:

    int x = 10;

Here:

    x

designates an object.


This terminology becomes important when learning:

    pointers
    memory management
    arrays
    structures
    const
    volatile
    atomic types


------------------------------------------------------------
48. SCOPE OF A VARIABLE
------------------------------------------------------------

Scope means where an identifier can be accessed in source code.

Example:

    #include <stdio.h>

    int main(void)
    {
        int x = 10;

        printf("%d\n", x);

        return 0;
    }


x exists within the scope of main's block.


------------------------------------------------------------
49. BLOCK SCOPE
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    int main(void)
    {
        int x = 10;

        {
            int y = 20;

            printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
        }

        // y cannot be accessed here

        return 0;
    }


x is accessible in the inner block.

y is only accessible inside the inner block.


------------------------------------------------------------
50. GLOBAL VARIABLE
------------------------------------------------------------

A variable declared outside all functions has file scope.

Example:

    int counter = 0;

    int main(void)
    {
        counter++;
        return 0;
    }


Global variables can be accessed by functions according to
their linkage/scope rules.


Use global state carefully because excessive global state can
make software harder to test and maintain.


------------------------------------------------------------
51. LOCAL VARIABLE
------------------------------------------------------------

A variable declared inside a function/block is commonly
called a local variable.

Example:

    void test(void)
    {
        int x = 10;
    }


x is local to that block.


------------------------------------------------------------
52. VARIABLE LIFETIME
------------------------------------------------------------

Lifetime means the period during program execution for which
an object exists.

Important concepts:

    scope      → where the name can be used
    lifetime   → how long the object exists
    linkage    → whether declarations refer to the same entity
                 across scopes/files


Do not confuse these three.


------------------------------------------------------------
53. AUTOMATIC VARIABLES
------------------------------------------------------------

Local variables normally have automatic storage duration.

Example:

    void test(void)
    {
        int x = 10;
    }


x is created when execution enters its block and ceases to
exist when execution leaves the block.


------------------------------------------------------------
54. STATIC VARIABLE
------------------------------------------------------------

A local static variable retains its value between function
calls.

Example:

    #include <stdio.h>

    void counter(void)
    {
        static int count = 0;

        count++;

        printf("%d\n", count);
    }

    int main(void)
    {
        counter();
        counter();
        counter();

        return 0;
    }

Output:

    1
    2
    3


Unlike an automatic local variable, count has static storage
duration.


------------------------------------------------------------
55. EXTERNAL VARIABLE
------------------------------------------------------------

The extern keyword can declare an object defined elsewhere.

Example:

    extern int counter;


It tells the compiler that the definition is provided
elsewhere.

Useful in multi-file programs.


------------------------------------------------------------
56. VARIABLE INITIALIZATION RULE
------------------------------------------------------------

Automatic local variable:

    int x;

does not automatically become 0.

Static-storage-duration objects are initialized to zero if no
explicit initializer is provided.

Example:

    static int count;

count starts as:

    0


Global/file-scope objects also receive zero initialization
when no explicit initializer is provided.


------------------------------------------------------------
57. TYPE CONVERSION PREVIEW
------------------------------------------------------------

Different data types can interact.

Example:

    int a = 10;
    double b = 2.5;

    double result = a + b;


The int value is converted for the operation.


Type conversion will be studied deeply in Chapter 6.


------------------------------------------------------------
58. INTEGER PROMOTION
------------------------------------------------------------

Small integer types such as char and short are commonly
promoted to int (or unsigned int where required) during many
expressions.

Example:

    char a = 10;
    char b = 20;

    int result = a + b;


The expression:

    a + b

does not normally perform char-sized arithmetic.


This is important in interviews.


------------------------------------------------------------
59. INTEGER OVERFLOW
------------------------------------------------------------

Signed integer overflow is a serious issue.

Example:

    int x = INT_MAX;

    x = x + 1;


This causes undefined behavior for signed int arithmetic.


Unsigned integer arithmetic behaves differently:

    unsigned int x = UINT_MAX;

    x = x + 1;


The result wraps modulo 2^N for an N-bit unsigned type.


------------------------------------------------------------
60. DATA TYPE SELECTION
------------------------------------------------------------

Choose a type based on the data and required range.

Examples:

Age:

    int age;

Employee count:

    unsigned int employee_count;

Very large ID/count:

    unsigned long long id;

Decimal scientific calculation:

    double value;

Single character:

    char grade;


Do NOT select types only because they "look small."


------------------------------------------------------------
61. FIXED-WIDTH INTEGER TYPES
------------------------------------------------------------

For systems programming, exact-width integer types are useful.

Header:

    #include <stdint.h>


Examples:

    int8_t
    int16_t
    int32_t
    int64_t

Unsigned:

    uint8_t
    uint16_t
    uint32_t
    uint64_t


Example:

    int32_t employee_id = 1001;


Important:

These types are provided when the implementation supports
the requested exact width.


------------------------------------------------------------
62. SIZE TYPES
------------------------------------------------------------

size_t is an unsigned integer type used for sizes/counts
where appropriate.

Example:

    size_t length = 100;


It is commonly used with:

    sizeof
    array/string lengths
    memory allocation


Example:

    size_t size = sizeof(int);


------------------------------------------------------------
63. FORMAT SPECIFIERS
------------------------------------------------------------

When using printf, the format specifier should match the type.

Examples:

    int:
        %d

    unsigned int:
        %u

    long:
        %ld

    long long:
        %lld

    unsigned long long:
        %llu

    float:
        %f

    double:
        %f

    char:
        %c

    string:
        %s

    size_t:
        %zu


For portable integer types such as int32_t, use the macros
from <inttypes.h> when appropriate.


------------------------------------------------------------
64. COMMON FORMAT SPECIFIER MISTAKES
------------------------------------------------------------

Example:

    long long x = 100;

Use:

    printf("%lld\n", x);


Do not randomly use:

    %d

for every integer type.

The format specifier must correspond appropriately to the
argument type expected by printf.


